WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.881
Welcome to Nabulines Academy.

2
00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:04.087
You've got Claude Code installed.

3
00:00:04.725 --> 00:00:06.153
Now let's actually use it to build

4
00:00:06.211 --> 00:00:06.734
something.

5
00:00:07.372 --> 00:00:09.195
In this lesson, you'll start Claude Code

6
00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:11.505
in a folder, ask it to create a script,

7
00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:12.318
and run it.

8
00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:14.779
By the end, you'll have your first real

9
00:00:14.838 --> 00:00:16.149
project built with AI.

10
00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:17.508
Let's go.

11
00:00:17.508 --> 00:00:18.565
Open your terminal.

12
00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:20.805
Create a new folder for your project.

13
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:23.650
Type mkdir my-first-project.

14
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:24.904
Hit enter.

15
00:00:25.542 --> 00:00:27.237
Now move into that folder with cd

16
00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:28.537
my-first-project.

17
00:00:29.281 --> 00:00:30.906
This is where everything will live.

18
00:00:31.777 --> 00:00:33.878
Now type claude-code and hit enter.

19
00:00:34.621 --> 00:00:36.339
Claude Code starts up in the current

20
00:00:36.385 --> 00:00:36.885
folder.

21
00:00:37.628 --> 00:00:39.416
It opens a chat interface right in your

22
00:00:39.451 --> 00:00:39.973
terminal.

23
00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:41.285
You're ready to build.

24
00:00:41.285 --> 00:00:42.330
Now talk to Claude.

25
00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:44.524
Type your request in plain English.

26
00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:46.811
Let's ask it to create a script that

27
00:00:46.869 --> 00:00:48.948
generates random motivational quotes.

28
00:00:49.586 --> 00:00:52.291
Type: Create a script that prints a random

29
00:00:52.349 --> 00:00:54.323
motivational quote each time I run it.

30
00:00:54.962 --> 00:00:55.588
Hit enter.

31
00:00:56.227 --> 00:00:58.247
Claude Code reads your request.

32
00:00:58.885 --> 00:00:59.791
It thinks for a moment.

33
00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:02.345
Then it creates a file called quotes dot j

34
00:01:02.391 --> 00:01:02.786
s.

35
00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:04.086
It writes the code.

36
00:01:04.307 --> 00:01:05.573
It explains what it did.

37
00:01:06.443 --> 00:01:07.825
You don't have to write a single line

38
00:01:07.848 --> 00:01:08.638
yourself.

39
00:01:08.638 --> 00:01:10.078
Let's look at what Claude built.

40
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:12.272
Open quotes dot j s.

41
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.826
There's an array of motivational quotes.

42
00:01:15.465 --> 00:01:17.229
There's code to pick one at random.

43
00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:19.331
There's a console dot log to print it.

44
00:01:20.074 --> 00:01:22.477
Claude wrote real, working JavaScript.

45
00:01:23.348 --> 00:01:25.043
You didn't have to know the syntax.

46
00:01:25.682 --> 00:01:27.237
You didn't have to look up how to generate

47
00:01:27.284 --> 00:01:28.155
random numbers.

48
00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:30.511
You just described what you wanted.

49
00:01:30.511 --> 00:01:31.289
Now run it.

50
00:01:32.032 --> 00:01:33.948
Type node quotes dot j s.

51
00:01:34.238 --> 00:01:34.923
Hit enter.

52
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:36.769
A random quote appears.

53
00:01:37.512 --> 00:01:38.290
Run it again.

54
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.300
Different quote.

55
00:01:39.648 --> 00:01:41.517
You just built a working script by asking

56
00:01:41.575 --> 00:01:41.993
for it.

57
00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:43.607
This is vibe coding.

58
00:01:43.955 --> 00:01:45.186
You describe the vibe.

59
00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:47.148
Claude writes the code.

60
00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:49.226
You run it and see it work.

61
00:01:49.226 --> 00:01:50.457
Want to change something?

62
00:01:50.747 --> 00:01:51.560
Just ask.

63
00:01:51.815 --> 00:01:53.069
Go back to Claude Code.

64
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.296
Type: Add five more quotes to the list.

65
00:01:56.517 --> 00:01:57.841
Claude updates the file.

66
00:01:58.003 --> 00:01:58.781
Run it again.

67
00:01:58.944 --> 00:01:59.803
More variety.

68
00:01:59.965 --> 00:02:01.533
You're iterating in plain English.

69
00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.949
No syntax errors.

70
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:04.052
No debugging.

71
00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:06.223
Just conversation and results.

72
00:02:06.223 --> 00:02:07.326
Here's what just happened.

73
00:02:07.964 --> 00:02:09.973
You started Claude Code in a folder.

74
00:02:10.716 --> 00:02:12.330
You described what you wanted in plain

75
00:02:12.388 --> 00:02:12.899
English.

76
00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:14.838
Claude created a real script.

77
00:02:15.302 --> 00:02:15.987
You ran it.

78
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:17.148
It worked.

79
00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:19.040
You asked for changes.

80
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:20.608
Claude made them.

81
00:02:21.153 --> 00:02:22.268
This is the workflow.

82
00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:25.287
Describe, build, run, iterate.

83
00:02:26.157 --> 00:02:28.236
No prior coding knowledge required.

84
00:02:28.236 --> 00:02:29.954
You just built your first project with

85
00:02:30.012 --> 00:02:30.721
Claude Code.

86
00:02:31.011 --> 00:02:31.986
A working script.

87
00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.414
Created by AI.

88
00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:34.587
Run by you.

89
00:02:34.935 --> 00:02:35.841
Now it's your turn.

90
00:02:36.386 --> 00:02:37.512
Open the exercise.

91
00:02:38.151 --> 00:02:40.368
Ask Claude Code to create a small script

92
00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:41.019
of your own.

93
00:02:41.657 --> 00:02:42.122
Run it.

94
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.515
See it work.

95
00:02:44.385 --> 00:02:46.301
Next lesson, we'll build something

96
00:02:46.359 --> 00:02:48.472
bigger—a multi-file project.

97
00:02:49.111 --> 00:02:50.016
See you there.
